A||N||3||BlackARROW||Valve ajustment? Or just Springs?||||1||
Z||000000||BlackARROW||09-21-2001||01:52 AM||||I have a '85 Iron Duked 4-cylinder, 202,000km, doesn't burn oil at all (in fact, I have to change my oil, and it's still full - didn't drop a millimeter), for some times, the motor is kind of clicking, especially when cold..  Under warmer condition, it seems to dim, but you open the trunk and it's still there, of course. Probably because I ride too much on highspeed mode - who knows  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>One mecanic told me that it is my one or some valves that would need to be reajusted.  Any Iron Duke expert?  I have never done this, and was wondering if first it was possible, and how.  'Cause I asked my brother, and he told me a mechanic would charge me as much as 600$ca (about 375$us) to change all of them, maybe including the springs and rockers?<P>Thanks in adv.<P>BlackAR-&gt;OW<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leadfoot||09-21-2001||04:49 PM||||I rebuilt a duke a couple of years ago and if memory serves me I don't think the valve lash is adjustable.  On the 2.8 you actual can adjust the valve lash but on the 2.5 I think you just torque the valve stud nut down to something like 25 ft-lbs.  I am sure I did this on my 2.5 s10 blazer at 80k and it quieted down a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fotofrank||09-21-2001||05:22 PM||||The duke valve lash is not adjustable. If you have the original head the bolt holding the rocker should be torqued to about 25ftlbs. I say should cause ware of the cam, lifters, pushrods and rocker could cause the required torque to change. If the only problem is some chater I don't think I waould worry. If the rocker bolt is too loose the rocker slips off the push rod( I did this boy is it scarry to hear). If the torque is too high the car will not even start (I did this too). As long as you do not back fire, get good milage and have decent power do not tighten the rocker bolts. If you like, take the valve cover off(use a blue goop as a gasket when replacing) and check the "feel" of each rocker. Some may "feel" looser thn others but are most likely OK. That is some rockers may move a bit but not really loose contact with the lifter and others not move at all. This depends on the location of the cam when the engine stops. Good luck. <BR>  The real problem may be the chanels and holes in the push rods and rockers, for oil, are cloged. Befor next oil change flush by adding a quart of clearner from you local auto parts store.<BR>  Let us know what you find and how it was fixed.<BR>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4 <A HREF="http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html</A> <P>[This message has been edited by fotofrank (edited 09-21-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by fotofrank (edited 09-21-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000003||BlackARROW||09-27-2001||12:24 AM||||I did my oil change today, and used a common STP Engine Flush to clean it up.  Ahhh...<BR>Well, it looks like it's still clicking, and I am not surprised.  <P>According to the "Fiero Online Service Guide", the solution to the hydraulic valve clicking noise is to change the lifters:  "The best thing to do is to either ignore the problem until it gets worse (like clicking hard all the time), or replace all of the lifters." (http://thefieroclub.com/osg/fiero_osg/lifters.html)<P>I think I will do as it says, ignore the noise for now, but will think about changing them (as it seems to be quite easily to do)<P><P>------------------<BR>BlackARROW &gt;&gt;--&gt;<BR>Gadgeteered Fiero 2M4 '85<BR>"Get your groove on"||||reg||1
